---
/**
 * The answer to the question readers bring: can one satellite image see what is claimed under Khafre? Built from the
 * claim computed for its own geometry (P2-38), the published method read blind on images of known ground (P2-37) and the
 * proof's bounds; every number is the runs' own. A statement, three numbers, what it rests on, what could change it.
 */
import claim from '../../public/data/runs/p2_38_claim.json';
import read from '../../public/data/runs/p2_37_benchmark_read.json';

interface Props { heading?: 'h2' | 'h3' }
const { heading = 'h2' } = Astro.props;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

type ClRow = { l1: Record<string, { tv_upper: number }>; oracle_power_weighted: Record<string, { tv_upper: number }>; l1_growth_to_target: number | null; oracle_growth_to_target: number };
type ClRows = Record<string, ClRow>;
type ClPass = { headline_db: number; parts: Record<string, { rows: Record<string, ClRow> }> };
const cl25 = (claim as unknown as { passes: Record<string, ClPass> }).passes['giza-20250827'];
const key = String(cl25.headline_db);
const row = cl25.parts['claim (both)'].rows['as computed'];
// the margins after every allowance (a louder local ground, site amplification and a cube's larger moment, the
// background scaled too): the last row of the claim's table
const allowed = Object.values(cl25.parts['claim (both)'].rows as ClRows).slice(-1)[0];
const orGrowth = row.oracle_growth_to_target;
const rd = (read as unknown as { summary: Record<string, unknown>; manifest: { params: { grounds: number[] } } });
const nGrounds = rd.manifest.params.grounds.length;

const times = (v: number) => Number(v.toPrecision(2)).toLocaleString('en-US');
const cl22 = (claim as unknown as { passes: Record<string, ClPass> }).passes['giza-20220715'];
// the best equal-prior accuracy, 1/2 + TV/2, of a reader told every scatterer's reflectivity (the oracle, weighted by the
// image's measured brightness): a bound averaged over the modelled ensemble, not a measurement on the files
const acc = (tv: number) => `${(100 * (0.5 + tv / 2)).toFixed(tv < 2e-3 ? 3 : 2).replace(/0+$/, '').replace(/\.$/, '')}%`;
const or25 = row.oracle_power_weighted[key].tv_upper;
const or22 = cl22?.parts['claim (both)'].rows['as computed'].oracle_power_weighted[String(cl22.headline_db)]?.tv_upper;
type RdN = { named_of_three: number; images: number };
const blind = (rd.summary['x1000'] as unknown as { published_blind: RdN; told: RdN });
const nBlind = Math.round(blind.published_blind.named_of_three * blind.published_blind.images);
const nTold = Math.round(blind.told.named_of_three * blind.told.images);
const nImg = blind.published_blind.images;
const H = heading;
---
<div class="claim-verdict">
  <p class="cv-label">The question readers bring</p>
  <H class="cv-title">Can vibration processing of one radar image map the structures claimed beneath Khafre? Our assessment: no, under the conditions modelled here</H>
  <p class="cv-lede">
    We modelled representative shafts and a deep chamber at the claimed depths. Their vibration signatures are too weak
    for reliable recovery, even for an ideal reader given the surface’s reflectivity. Our implementation of the published
    method also failed to recover the tested layouts when their signals were greatly amplified.
  </p>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><span class="value">{acc(or25)}</span><span class="what">the best any reading of one image of Khafre could do at saying whether the claimed shafts and void are there, against 50% by guessing, even for a reader given every scatterer’s reflectivity{or22 != null ? ` (the 2022 image: ${acc(or22)})` : ''}</span><span class="src">P2-38 · a bound over the modelled ensemble, not a measurement on the files</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><span class="value">{times(orGrowth)}<small>×</small></span><span class="what">how much more the claimed structures would have to move the ground before that reader could find them reliably (95% found at 5% false alarms)</span><span class="src">P2-38 · most favourable specified noise floor</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><span class="value">{nBlind} of {nImg}</span><span class="what">layouts our implementation of the published method named correctly, with its stated readout, on simulated images of known ground with the cavities’ effect amplified a thousandfold: chance. A detector given the candidate patterns named {nTold} of {nImg}</span><span class="src">P2-37 · blind · {nGrounds} grounds · a diagnostic of this implementation</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="cv-cols">
    <div>
      <p class="cv-sub">What the assessment rests on</p>
      <p>Calculations on a model of the ground, not field measurements: uniform limestone, the trembling measured 67 km
      away, the radar maker’s most favourable noise figure, representative structures at the claimed depths; the pyramid is
      omitted, the direction of its effect not established. The physical bound covers every way of reading one image within
      that model, the published method included; the test of the method itself is of our implementation from the paper, not
      the authors’ software. The 2022 paper was retracted in August 2026.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="cv-sub">What could still change it</p>
      <p>Not a louder ground nearby: with the trembling ten times the level measured 67 km away, amplified three times by the
      site and a cube’s larger moment, the structures are still {times(allowed.l1_growth_to_target ?? NaN)} times short for one
      image and {times(allowed.oracle_growth_to_target)} times for the told reader. What could: a mechanism nobody has yet
      specified that makes the ground move thousands of times more over a void; a bright fixture over a cavity beside strong
      shaking, an important unresolved case within the benchmark; or a method shown to find surveyed voids blind, on ground
      withheld from it. None has been shown.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="cv-links"><a href={`${base}/proof/`}>The proof, finite and stated →</a> · <a href={`${base}/satellite/`}>every test, on the satellite page →</a></p>
</div>

<style>
  .claim-verdict {
    max-width: var(--page, 1040px);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .cv-label {
    font: 600 10.5px/1.4 var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.7px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .cv-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(22px, 3vw, 30px);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    max-width: 30ch;
  }
  h3.cv-title {
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 24px);
    max-width: 40ch;
  }
  .cv-lede {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--body);
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .cv-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 22px;
    margin-top: 16px;
  }
  .cv-cols p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--body);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cv-cols .cv-sub {
    font: 600 10.5px/1.4 var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.6px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 4px;
  }
  .cv-links {
    font: 12px var(--font-mono);
    margin: 14px 0 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .cv-cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .stat-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
